// tests/x509_api_tests.nv — the public surface, called on documents that
// really answer.
//
// The five suites the interface release landed are its own oracle:
// they pin the design and the refusals.  This one, and the edge suite
// beside it, are the other
// half — the certificates, lists and requests that carry the structure
// in question, so that every reader this package publishes is run
// against a document it must ACCEPT rather than only against ones it
// must refuse.  A suite whose fixtures are all refusals tests half a
// parser.
//
// The documents are hand-built DER, one per case, and each differs
// from a well-formed one by exactly the thing under test.  The four
// facts this package turns on are pinned here in the places they
// matter:
//
//   * RFC 5280 writes an entry's `crlEntryExtensions` UNTAGGED inside
//     the entry, while the LIST's own `crlExtensions` is `[0]
//     EXPLICIT` — the two are not written the same way and a reader
//     that assumed so dropped every reason and every invalidity date.
//   * a CRL's currency window is closed at BOTH ends, and the instant
//     is the caller's argument: one list, four moments, four answers.
//   * a name CONSTRAINT compares by the X.500 rule — case folded,
//     whitespace collapsed — while chain building compares name
//     ENCODINGS.  Both are pinned, and they disagree on purpose.
//   * `[1] issuerUniqueID` and `[2] subjectUniqueID` are BIT STRINGs
//     whose universal tag was replaced, so X.690's rules for the type
//     are applied by hand and a constructed one or an eighth unused
//     bit is refused.

use std.test
use std.bytes
use asn1read
use asn1err
use asn1pkix
use asn1oid
use civil
use x509cert
use x509crl
use x509csr
use x509ext
use x509name
use x509path
use x509err
use x509verify

// The bounds every read here is made under: depth 64, a 16 MiB value,
// DER strictly.
fn limits() -> AsnLimits
    asn1read.default_limits()

// ── the documents ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The DER documents this suite reads, one function each.
//
// Written by hand, byte by byte, so that each differs from a
// well-formed document by exactly the thing the test above it names.
// `bytes.from_hex` is the one constructor a `core` package has, so
// each is a hexadecimal literal and nothing else.

